1
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:05,090
Okay, Babydoll Pink,

2
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:08,120
let's see if you can cover up the fact that I got my dad's feet.

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:10,970
It's just two degrees, Sheldon.

4
00:00:10,990 --> 00:00:13,050
I just want to turn up the thermostat two degrees!

5
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:14,910
Let me point out that two degrees

6
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:17,380
can be the difference between water and steam.

7
00:00:17,410 --> 00:00:19,460
Yes, if we lived in a teakettle.

8
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:22,420
This is the temperature you agreed to in the roommate agreement.

9
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:24,620
Aw, screw the roommate agreement!

10
00:00:24,630 --> 00:00:26,560
No, you don't screw the roommate agreement.

11
00:00:26,590 --> 00:00:28,570
The roommate agreement screws you.

12
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:33,040
You know what, go to hell and set their thermostat.

13
00:00:33,050 --> 00:00:34,350
I don't have to go to hell.

14
00:00:34,390 --> 00:00:37,230
At 73 degrees, I'm there already!

15
00:00:40,770 --> 00:00:41,930
Who is it?

16
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:44,320
Leonard.

17
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:45,200
Hang on.

18
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:53,190
Can I sleep on your couch tonight?

19
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:54,620
Uh, well, you can try,

20
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:56,940
but the people across the hall are being very noisy.

21
00:00:57,890 --> 00:00:59,040
You heard that, huh?

22
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:02,020
Apparently, the one fella tried to adjust the thermostat,

23
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:04,480
then the other fella went bat-crap crazy.

24
00:01:04,990 --> 00:01:07,050
So you agree, he's nuts.

25
00:01:07,090 --> 00:01:09,850
Well, not as nuts as the guy who chooses to live with him.

26
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:12,520
Believe it or not, he was worse when I met him.

27
00:01:12,590 --> 00:01:14,040
Oh, I do not believe that.

28
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:16,840
You are so naive.

29
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:19,510
Just like I was seven years ago.

30
00:01:20,890 --> 00:01:22,880
I'd just started at the university.

31
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:32,650
Excuse me, I'm looking for Sheldon Cooper's apartment.

32
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:34,380
Oh, I bet you're here to check out the room for rent.

33
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,090
- Yeah.  - Run away, dude.

34
00:01:38,210 --> 00:01:39,060
What?!

35
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:41,570
Run fast, run far.

36
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,310
That should have been my first clue.

37
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,570
So Sheldon's last roommate tried to warn you off?

38
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:17,350
For all I knew, he was the crazy one.

39
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:20,420
He had this really deranged look.

40
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:23,080
Well, yeah, he'd been living with Sheldon.

41
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:26,200
Sure, it makes sense now.

42
00:02:28,810 --> 00:02:31,010
Anyway, I went upstairs and knocked on the door.

43
00:02:37,850 --> 00:02:38,670
Yeah?

44
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:50,220
Dr. Cooper?

45
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:53,030
No, you want the crazy guy across the hall.

46
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:00,190
In retrospect, that was clue number two.

47
00:03:07,070 --> 00:03:07,870
Yes?

48
00:03:08,370 --> 00:03:09,260
I'm Leonard Hofstadter.

49
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:11,010
I called you about the apartment. You said...

50
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:11,960
I know what I said.

51
00:03:11,970 --> 00:03:12,860
I know what you said.

52
00:03:12,890 --> 00:03:15,640
I know what my mother said on March 5, 1992.

53
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:19,400
What is the sixth noble gas?

54
00:03:19,690 --> 00:03:21,750
- What?  - You said you're a scientist.

55
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:23,810
What is the sixth noble gas?

56
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:24,830
Uh, radon?

57
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:28,130
Are you asking me or telling me?

58
00:03:29,350 --> 00:03:30,220
Telling you?

59
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:34,730
Telling you.

60
00:03:35,250 --> 00:03:37,220
All right, next question.

61
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:38,880
Kirk or Picard?

62
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:41,410
Oh, uh, well, that's tricky.

63
00:03:42,730 --> 00:03:45,070
Um, Original Series over Next Generation,

64
00:03:45,090 --> 00:03:46,680
but Picard over Kirk.

65
00:03:47,410 --> 00:03:48,520
Correct.

66
00:03:49,710 --> 00:03:52,400
You've passed the first barrier to roommate-hood.

67
00:03:53,410 --> 00:03:54,610
You may enter.

68
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,520
Oh, this is pretty nice.

69
00:04:04,690 --> 00:04:06,450
Uh, the bedrooms are back there?

70
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:07,720
That depends.

71
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:08,920
I don't understand.

72
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:10,460
Their existence is conditional?

73
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:15,000
No, but your ability to perceive their existence is conditional

74
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,570
on you passing the second and third barriers.

75
00:04:17,570 --> 00:04:19,100
There's three?

76
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:21,120
Each more daunting than the last.

77
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:23,220
Have a seat.

78
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:24,090
Okay.

79
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:27,240
No! That's where I sit!

80
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:33,140
What's the difference?

81
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:35,110
This seat is ideally located

82
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:37,700
both in relation to the heat source in the winter

83
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:39,100
and a cross breeze in the summer.

84
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:41,810
It also faces the television at a direct angle,

85
00:04:41,810 --> 00:04:44,940
allowing me to immerse myself in entertainment or game play

86
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:46,860
without being subjected to conversation.

87
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:50,330
As a result, I've placed it in a state of eternal dibs.

88
00:04:52,570 --> 00:04:53,660
Can you do that?

89
00:04:54,490 --> 00:04:56,740
Cathedra mea, regulae meae.

90
00:04:58,280 --> 00:05:00,600
That's Latin for "My chair, my rules."

91
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:05,880
Now, you said on the phone

92
00:05:05,890 --> 00:05:08,570
that your area of study is physics.

93
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:10,370
Uh, yeah, experimental physics.

94
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:14,630
What is that?

95
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:15,830
Doesn't concern you.

96
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:18,580
You'll be going to the university every day?

97
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:19,360
Yes.

98
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:20,870
- And you have a vehicle?  - A car, yeah.

99
00:05:20,890 --> 00:05:22,280
And you'll be willing to drive me?

100
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:23,760
Can't you drive?

101
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:25,330
I can.

102
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:29,200
I choose not to.

103
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:32,930
Okay, I suppose I could drive you.

104
00:05:34,150 --> 00:05:35,690
That's a point in my favor, right?

105
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:37,340
Why don't you let me do this.

106
00:05:38,280 --> 00:05:39,980
Come on, I just asked.

107
00:05:44,210 --> 00:05:45,240
Last question.

108
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:47,240
In a post-apocalyptic world,

109
00:05:47,250 --> 00:05:49,800
which task would you assign the highest priority?

110
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:51,750
Locating a sustainable food source,

111
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:53,650
reestablishing a functioning government,

112
00:05:53,670 --> 00:05:56,420
procreating, or preserving the knowledge of mankind?

113
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:00,320
Uh, I'm gonna go with... preserving the knowledge.

114
00:06:00,350 --> 00:06:01,550
That's correct.

115
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:06,220
FYI, I would have accepted any answer other than procreating.

116
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:10,210
Come, I'll show you the rest of the apartment.

117
00:06:10,230 --> 00:06:11,190
Oh, good.

118
00:06:11,230 --> 00:06:12,770
I passed the barriers.

119
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:14,760
The second barrier. Don't get cocky.

120
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:19,050
This is the bathroom.

121
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:22,090
Are you fairly regular?

122
00:06:25,410 --> 00:06:26,780
Uh, I guess.

123
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:29,210
This isn't going to work if you're guessing.

124
00:06:30,090 --> 00:06:32,120
When do you evacuate your bowels?

125
00:06:33,570 --> 00:06:35,150
When I have to.

126
00:06:35,810 --> 00:06:37,000
When you have to?

127
00:06:37,010 --> 00:06:38,750
I'm sorry, I don't rent to hippies.

128
00:06:42,670 --> 00:06:44,670
I-I'm sorry, uh, in the morning.

129
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:45,740
Around 8:00.

130
00:06:45,770 --> 00:06:48,340
I can't give you 8:00. I can give you 7:30.

131
00:06:49,910 --> 00:06:51,160
Fine. I'll take it.

132
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:53,310
Third barrier passed.

133
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:56,520
You have won the right to see your room.

134
00:06:56,530 --> 00:06:57,420
Huzzah.

135
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:02,640
Is this it?

136
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:04,000
No, this is my room.

137
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:05,550
People don't go in my room.

138
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:07,840
So where do you sleep?

139
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:11,160
I don't understand.

140
00:07:11,710 --> 00:07:14,580
If people don't go in there, and you're people, and...

141
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:16,900
You are people, aren't you?

142
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:20,140
Making a joke.

143
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:21,400
Do you do this often?

144
00:07:22,790 --> 00:07:24,300
On occasion.

145
00:07:28,210 --> 00:07:29,480
Your room.

146
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:40,640
You may want to repaint.

147
00:07:42,540 --> 00:07:44,710
And after all that, you just moved in?

148
00:07:44,740 --> 00:07:46,580
I didn't just move in.

149
00:07:46,580 --> 00:07:48,530
First we had to iron out a few details.

150
00:07:48,540 --> 00:07:51,600
"Roommates agree that Friday nights shall be reserved

151
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:55,120
for watching Joss Whedon's brilliant new series Firefly."

152
00:07:57,050 --> 00:07:59,120
Does that really need to be in the agreement?

153
00:07:59,130 --> 00:08:02,240
We might as well settle it now; it's going to be on for years.

154
00:08:03,860 --> 00:08:05,080
Initial here.

155
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:10,750
All right, that's television and movies.

156
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:14,470
Section nine: miscellany.

157
00:08:14,830 --> 00:08:16,320
The apartment's flag

158
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:19,260
is a gold lion rampant on a field of azure.

159
00:08:20,830 --> 00:08:22,390
We have a flag?

160
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:34,240
Never fly it upside down.

161
00:08:35,470 --> 00:08:37,260
Unless the apartment's in distress.

162
00:08:38,010 --> 00:08:38,930
And next.

163
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:42,010
"If either of us ever invents time travel,

164
00:08:42,050 --> 00:08:44,770
"We agree our first stop will be this meeting today

165
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:46,810
in precisely five seconds."

166
00:08:49,310 --> 00:08:50,290
Okay.

167
00:09:02,190 --> 00:09:04,010
That's disappointing.

168
00:09:05,270 --> 00:09:07,830
Why on earth did you agree to all that?

169
00:09:07,870 --> 00:09:09,890
It was the best apartment I'd seen,

170
00:09:09,910 --> 00:09:11,360
the rent was very reasonable

171
00:09:11,370 --> 00:09:13,760
and after you've passed the first three barriers

172
00:09:13,790 --> 00:09:16,330
you kind of want to take it all the way.

173
00:09:16,910 --> 00:09:18,590
Well, I'm sorry, Leonard,

174
00:09:18,630 --> 00:09:20,790
it's very hard to feel sympathy for you.

175
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:21,690
Okay, how about this?

176
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:24,180
Let me tell you about the first time I brought a girl over.

177
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:26,370
Leonard?

178
00:09:27,430 --> 00:09:29,450
Shh. Just pretend we're not here.

179
00:09:32,010 --> 00:09:33,210
Leonard?

180
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:36,400
I'm sure he'll go away.

181
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:40,980
I'm just going to keep knocking till you answer.

182
00:09:41,960 --> 00:09:43,060
Leonard?

183
00:09:43,060 --> 00:09:44,530
Leonard?

184
00:09:44,530 --> 00:09:45,110
Leonard?

185
00:09:45,110 --> 00:09:46,200
What do you want?!

186
00:09:46,910 --> 00:09:48,690
I didn't say come in!

187
00:09:48,700 --> 00:09:50,310
You asked what I wanted.

188
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:52,940
I wanted to come in.

189
00:09:53,710 --> 00:09:56,340
I'm here because you violated our roommate agreement.

190
00:09:56,380 --> 00:09:58,130
Specifically, section eight--

191
00:09:58,170 --> 00:10:02,010
visitors, subsection C; females, paragraph four-- coitus.

192
00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:04,850
"Roommates shall give each other

193
00:10:04,880 --> 00:10:07,010
12 hours notice of impending coitus."

194
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:10,250
I didn't even know her 12 hours ago.

195
00:10:10,260 --> 00:10:12,240
That's it! I'm out of here!

196
00:10:12,270 --> 00:10:13,770
But, Joyce, come on.

197
00:10:13,810 --> 00:10:15,350
12 hours?

198
00:10:17,410 --> 00:10:19,390
Oh, my God.

199
00:10:19,610 --> 00:10:21,160
Do I get some sympathy now?

200
00:10:21,630 --> 00:10:22,850
A little bit.

201
00:10:27,370 --> 00:10:28,570
Ok, let me get this straight.

202
00:10:28,580 --> 00:10:29,930
You move in with this guy,

203
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:32,090
he made you sign a rediculous roommate agreement,

204
00:10:32,340 --> 00:10:33,690
then he walks into your bedroom

205
00:10:33,710 --> 00:10:35,370
while you're doing this Joyce Kim

206
00:10:35,380 --> 00:10:36,410
and you still stay.

207
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:39,190
Actually, I couldn't get too mad at him about Joyce Kim.

208
00:10:39,190 --> 00:10:40,530
Why not?

209
00:10:40,530 --> 00:10:43,130
Well, I was doing some government research at the time,

210
00:10:43,130 --> 00:10:45,030
you know, military, rocket fuel.

211
00:10:45,030 --> 00:10:46,300
It's kind of secret.

212
00:10:46,710 --> 00:10:48,100
What does that have to do with Joyce Kim?

213
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:51,400
As it turns out, she was a North Korean spy.

214
00:10:53,620 --> 00:10:54,770
Luckily, Sheldon drove her out

215
00:10:54,770 --> 00:10:57,070
before I revealed anything important.

216
00:10:57,110 --> 00:10:59,540
Which I-I'm not saying I would have.

217
00:11:00,850 --> 00:11:02,170
So, what, that's it?

218
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:03,520
You've stayed with Sheldon all this time

219
00:11:03,530 --> 00:11:05,740
because he kept you from going to federal prison?

220
00:11:06,210 --> 00:11:07,350
That was part of it.

221
00:11:07,470 --> 00:11:09,790
The other part is what happened with the elevator.

222
00:11:09,830 --> 00:11:11,260
Oh, yeah, I'm wondering about that.

223
00:11:11,290 --> 00:11:12,870
You said it was working when you moved in.

224
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:15,920
It was, but one night, Sheldon came home from work...

225
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:28,210
What is going on here?

226
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:29,990
Hey, Sheldon.

227
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:30,840
This is Howard and Raj.

228
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:32,140
They work at the university, too.

229
00:11:32,170 --> 00:11:33,080
- Hey. - Hey.

230
00:11:33,090 --> 00:11:34,130
I'll get to you later.

231
00:11:35,970 --> 00:11:37,460
What are you sitting on?

232
00:11:37,470 --> 00:11:40,400
I can't speak for these guys, but I'm sitting on my tushie.

233
00:11:42,190 --> 00:11:43,230
It's a joke.

234
00:11:43,710 --> 00:11:44,960
Not a good idea.

235
00:11:45,910 --> 00:11:47,140
"Tushie" Is buttocks, right?

236
00:11:47,180 --> 00:11:49,350
- Right. - Hilarious.

237
00:11:50,710 --> 00:11:52,210
Explain the couch.

238
00:11:52,230 --> 00:11:54,370
Oh, well, there were some people on the first floor moving out,

239
00:11:54,370 --> 00:11:55,770
and they sold it to me for $100.

240
00:11:55,770 --> 00:11:57,410
Howard and Raj helped me bring it up.

241
00:11:57,410 --> 00:12:00,410
But what's wrong with the furniture we have?

242
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:02,560
They're lawn chairs.

243
00:12:03,690 --> 00:12:05,490
And there was no place for company.

244
00:12:05,550 --> 00:12:07,910
Did it occur to you that was by design?

245
00:12:09,310 --> 00:12:10,770
According to the roommate agreement,

246
00:12:10,790 --> 00:12:14,510
I'm entitled to allocate 50% of the cubic footage of the common areas.

247
00:12:14,540 --> 00:12:17,850
But you didn't notify me by e-mail, so this is still a breach.

248
00:12:19,050 --> 00:12:20,500
I did notify you.

249
00:12:20,510 --> 00:12:22,460
Oh, you did, did you?

250
00:12:37,550 --> 00:12:39,320
Oh, drat!

251
00:12:41,190 --> 00:12:43,900
Hoisted by my own spam filter.

252
00:12:44,790 --> 00:12:46,940
What am I doing in your spam folder?

253
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:48,310
I put you there after you forwarded me

254
00:12:48,310 --> 00:12:51,750
a picture of a cat playing the piano, entitled "This is funny."

255
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:54,020
Oh, yeah, I saw that.

256
00:12:54,030 --> 00:12:55,490
That was hilarious.

257
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:58,450
Okay, what does all this have to do with the elevator?

258
00:12:58,490 --> 00:12:59,740
I'm getting to it.

259
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:03,890
I assure you, you'll be sorry you wasted your money on an iPod

260
00:13:03,910 --> 00:13:05,990
when Microsoft comes out with theirs.

261
00:13:08,560 --> 00:13:10,780
Okay, do you have an opinion about everything?

262
00:13:11,250 --> 00:13:12,230
Yes.

263
00:13:13,940 --> 00:13:16,350
And you just assume you're always right?

264
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:18,480
It's not an assumption.

265
00:13:20,100 --> 00:13:21,550
Change seats with me.

266
00:13:21,550 --> 00:13:23,220
- Why? - I don't like this spot.

267
00:13:23,220 --> 00:13:24,680
I have to keep turning my head.

268
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:26,390
Fine.

269
00:13:27,780 --> 00:13:29,740
Ooh, it's time for Babylon 5!

270
00:13:29,970 --> 00:13:32,470
We don't watch Babylon 5 in this apartment.

271
00:13:32,480 --> 00:13:33,320
Why not?

272
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:35,390
Because no one likes Babylon 5.

273
00:13:35,600 --> 00:13:36,720
I like it.

274
00:13:36,740 --> 00:13:37,990
- Me, too. - So do I.

275
00:13:38,030 --> 00:13:39,350
There you go-- three against one.

276
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:40,630
They don't get a vote.

277
00:13:40,660 --> 00:13:41,810
It's one against one.

278
00:13:41,830 --> 00:13:43,480
And according to the roommate agreement,

279
00:13:43,490 --> 00:13:45,760
all ties will be settled by me.

280
00:13:47,290 --> 00:13:48,640
But I said no to that.

281
00:13:48,950 --> 00:13:50,100
And I said yes.

282
00:13:51,380 --> 00:13:53,130
And I settle all ties.

283
00:13:57,460 --> 00:13:58,880
Change seats with me.

284
00:13:58,880 --> 00:13:59,760
Why?

285
00:13:59,800 --> 00:14:01,490
There's a draft on my neck over here.

286
00:14:01,510 --> 00:14:03,160
So, I get the draft?

287
00:14:03,180 --> 00:14:04,880
You're protected by your turtleneck.

288
00:14:07,490 --> 00:14:08,450
Fine.

289
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:11,520
And it's a dickey.

290
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:23,550
I'm still not comfortable.

291
00:14:24,490 --> 00:14:25,280
Of course.

292
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:26,480
There's too many people here.

293
00:14:28,250 --> 00:14:29,420
We can fix that.

294
00:14:29,460 --> 00:14:30,500
Let's leave.

295
00:14:31,950 --> 00:14:33,800
Yeah, we can go over to my place.

296
00:14:33,830 --> 00:14:34,710
Wait.

297
00:14:34,750 --> 00:14:35,980
Let me get my jacket.

298
00:14:38,010 --> 00:14:40,440
- You're not going with us. - Why not?

299
00:14:40,470 --> 00:14:42,910
You're the guy we're trying to get away from.

300
00:14:45,830 --> 00:14:47,670
Well, in that case, I don't need my jacket.

301
00:14:48,810 --> 00:14:50,930
And for the record, the correct syntax is:

302
00:14:50,940 --> 00:14:53,090
I'm the guy from whom you're trying to get away.

303
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:04,080
Oh, yes.

304
00:15:07,950 --> 00:15:10,490
This is definitely going to be my spot.

305
00:15:11,560 --> 00:15:12,100
Okay.

306
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:14,190
How do you know he said that? You left the room.

307
00:15:14,210 --> 00:15:17,120
Hey, do you want me to finish working on your man feet or not?

308
00:15:18,890 --> 00:15:20,360
Fine. Go ahead.

309
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:24,630
Howard, are you having a playdate?

310
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:26,440
I don't have playdates!

311
00:15:26,440 --> 00:15:28,310
I have colleagues!

312
00:15:28,310 --> 00:15:31,950
Do their parents know they're here?

313
00:15:32,350 --> 00:15:35,790
No, but if you keep screaming, maybe they'll hear you!

314
00:15:40,670 --> 00:15:41,790
That your dad?

315
00:15:44,350 --> 00:15:47,130
She grows any more hair on her face, yes.

316
00:15:48,820 --> 00:15:51,600
Oh, man. Is that a two-stage rocket?

317
00:15:51,620 --> 00:15:54,310
Three. I designed the engine myself.

318
00:15:54,710 --> 00:15:56,680
Cool. Can it break Mach 1?

319
00:15:56,710 --> 00:15:59,510
Oh, probably, if I could get my hands on that new fuel

320
00:15:59,550 --> 00:16:01,490
the government's been working on.

321
00:16:01,810 --> 00:16:03,630
Oh, this just might be your lucky day.

322
00:16:03,630 --> 00:16:08,470
Howard, what happened to the Oreos I left on the counter?!

323
00:16:09,680 --> 00:16:12,690
I haven't seen your Oreos!

324
00:16:13,270 --> 00:16:15,760
Just take your bath without them!

325
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,700
So, why was it his lucky day?

326
00:16:21,700 --> 00:16:23,970
Well, it turns out I had a little rocket fuel in the apartment.

327
00:16:24,680 --> 00:16:26,670
What were you doing with rocket fuel in your apartment?

328
00:16:26,670 --> 00:16:29,970
Joyce Kim was kind of curious about what I did for a living,

329
00:16:29,980 --> 00:16:32,400
and I was going to kind of show it to her.

330
00:16:34,610 --> 00:16:35,810
It's not important. The point is,

331
00:16:35,810 --> 00:16:37,850
the guys and I went back to the apartment.

332
00:16:37,850 --> 00:16:39,420
Are we ever going to get to the elevator?

333
00:16:39,420 --> 00:16:40,450
Yeah, we're really close.

334
00:16:40,450 --> 00:16:41,650
Uh, uh, we're at the apartment.

335
00:16:41,700 --> 00:16:45,230
The trick is to mix it into Tovex in order to create a combustible gel

336
00:16:45,230 --> 00:16:48,300
that will generate over 8,000 kilo-newtons of thrust.

337
00:16:48,300 --> 00:16:49,440
- Cool. - Nice.

338
00:16:49,440 --> 00:16:50,740
Won't work.

339
00:16:52,040 --> 00:16:54,680
Excuse me, but I've been working on this a long time.

340
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:56,010
Trust me-- it'll work.

341
00:16:56,590 --> 00:16:58,110
You don't see your mistake, do you?

342
00:16:58,670 --> 00:16:59,710
There's no mistake.

343
00:16:59,720 --> 00:17:02,450
This is for a full-scale rocket, not a model.

344
00:17:02,450 --> 00:17:04,090
Well, I've adjusted the formula.

345
00:17:04,500 --> 00:17:06,350
Not correctly.

346
00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:08,320
Okay, I've had it with you.

347
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:12,610
You might be an expert on theoretical physics and science-fiction programs

348
00:17:12,610 --> 00:17:14,260
and where to sit on a freaking couch,

349
00:17:14,260 --> 00:17:16,060
but this is applied physics.

350
00:17:16,060 --> 00:17:18,850
And when it comes to applied physic... uh-oh.

351
00:17:19,670 --> 00:17:20,840
What's happening?

352
00:17:20,840 --> 00:17:22,640
A bad thing. A very bad thing.

353
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:23,700
Get the door.

354
00:17:23,710 --> 00:17:25,210
Get the door! Get the door!

355
00:17:25,210 --> 00:17:27,580
Get the door! Get the door!

356
00:17:37,960 --> 00:17:39,690
You're waiting for the elevator?!

357
00:17:40,230 --> 00:17:41,560
Oh. Right.

358
00:17:42,350 --> 00:17:43,480
Wait. It's here.

359
00:17:47,560 --> 00:17:49,300
Give me that.

360
00:17:52,730 --> 00:17:54,500
What'd you do that for? I had plenty of time...

361
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:01,780
You're welcome.

362
00:18:05,130 --> 00:18:06,510
Not only did Sheldon save my life,

363
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:08,350
he didn't rat me out to the landlord.

364
00:18:08,350 --> 00:18:09,520
Or the police.

365
00:18:09,520 --> 00:18:11,820
Or Homeland Security.

366
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:13,690
Okay, so, basically, you're the reason

367
00:18:13,690 --> 00:18:16,520
I have to walk up and down three flights of stairs every day?

368
00:18:16,530 --> 00:18:17,790
So I did something stupid.

369
00:18:17,790 --> 00:18:19,830
I'm sure you did stupid things when you were younger.

370
00:18:19,830 --> 00:18:21,360
What were you doing seven years ago?

371
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:23,000
Excuse me, I was in high school.

372
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,030
Studying, keeping my nose clean,

373
00:18:25,030 --> 00:18:27,170
doing volunteer work for the community.

374
00:18:30,210 --> 00:18:32,040
Not pregnant.

375
00:18:32,040 --> 00:18:33,580
Yes!

376
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:41,890
Oh, Ubuntu,

377
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:46,090
you are my favorite Linux-based operating system.

378
00:18:50,830 --> 00:18:52,430
Why do I smell methacrylate?

379
00:18:52,790 --> 00:18:55,700
Oh, uh, clear nail polish. I had a mani-pedi.

380
00:18:57,150 --> 00:18:58,600
Men can get those.

381
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:01,570
Anyway, I may owe you an apology.

382
00:19:02,590 --> 00:19:04,290
There's doubt?

383
00:19:04,810 --> 00:19:06,510
I did agree to the thermostat setting,

384
00:19:06,510 --> 00:19:08,650
and I shouldn't have tried to change it.

385
00:19:09,330 --> 00:19:10,550
That's not an apology.

386
00:19:10,550 --> 00:19:13,090
Simply an acknowledgement that I was right.

387
00:19:13,680 --> 00:19:14,890
Ok,I'm sorry.

388
00:19:14,910 --> 00:19:15,820
There you go.

389
00:19:17,490 --> 00:19:18,390
So we are good?

390
00:19:19,360 --> 00:19:20,240
Good what?

391
00:19:21,930 --> 00:19:22,870
Never mind.

392
00:19:23,210 --> 00:19:24,520
Okay if I watch some TV?

393
00:19:24,840 --> 00:19:25,800
Go ahead.

394
00:19:26,880 --> 00:19:29,210
Up next Babylon 5.

395
00:19:31,110 --> 00:19:32,510
You're not even watching!

396
00:19:32,510 --> 00:19:33,870
I can hear it!

397
00:19:33,890 --> 00:19:35,920
Oh, so the dialogue offends you?

398
00:19:35,930 --> 00:19:37,870
I would hardly call that dialogue!

399
00:19:37,900 --> 00:19:39,720
You're insane, you know that?!

400
00:19:39,730 --> 00:19:41,520
Don't make me turn that flag upside down,

401
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:42,830
'cause you know I'll do it!

